The temporal displacement tasted like crushed copper and burning frost.
When the chronal membrane shattered around Jose Lanz, the hum of the twenty-second century died in an instant. The hyper-luminescent towers of New Pamplona, the pressurized skylanes, and the towering geo-thermal vents of 2150 were ripped away, replaced by the deafening, primordial silence of the Pyrenees.
Jose collapsed onto damp, moss-choked earth. He inhaled sharply, his lungs burning with air untainted by synthetic scrubbers—raw, biting, and saturated with the scent of wild thyme, wet slate, and decaying pine.
He rose slowly, adjusting the kinetic weave of his matte-black environmental suit, which shimmered faintly as it adapted to the chill. Jose was a man forged of ancient and future blood: an olive-bronze complexion common to the enduring Basque-Navarrese lineage, a sharp, aquiline nose that mirrored the limestone crags of his ancestors, and thick, obsidian hair tied back from high, angular cheekbones. Beneath his heavy brow, dark eyes tracked the terrain, their irises ringed with the faint, glowing sapphire filaments of his neuro-optic implants.
The year was 1000.
He stood upon the jagged ridge of Mount Ezcaba, looking down into a valley untouched by civilization’s metallic sprawl. There were no magnetic transit tubes, no vaulted biospheres—only rolling ribbons of emerald forest, drifting gray mist, and the distant, fragile flicker of hearth fires from a stone settlement.
Jose had not made the forbidden jump to observe; he had come to cure his world.
In 2150, the entire Iberian Basin lived under the suffocating shadow of the Cinder Spire—a towering, indestructible obelisk of vitrified black alloy buried deep beneath the foundations of Navarre. For centuries, this impossible geological anomaly had bled a low-frequency, chronal-dampening radiation into the Earth’s crust. It rendered modern fusion power unstable across southern Europe, forcing billions to endure draconian energy rations dictated by the iron-fisted Consortium.
According to ancient monastic fragments recovered from the ashes of the Middle Ages, the blight began here, in the autumn of the year 1000, when a "fiery demon of black iron" fell from the heavens and struck the mountain hermitage, petrifying the land and cursing the lineage of the valley forever.
Jose carried the salvation of 2150 in the cylinder strapped to his back: an entropic null-cannon. If he could locate the celestial debris before the superstitious hermits consecrated it into the earth, he could disintegrate the meteor into harmless quantum dust. The anomaly would cease to exist. The Spire would never form. His people would be free.
Guided by the sub-dermal compass glowing beneath his wrist, Jose descended through the dense canopy. Beech leaves, heavy with autumn rain, whipped against his suit. The forest was alive with a guttural, terrifying vitality.
Within an hour, he reached a stone hollow. A crude, dry-stone chapel stood half-swallowed by the fog. Before it, huddled around a smoking crater, knelt a dozen figures cloaked in coarse, undyed wool and boiled leather. They were chanting in an archaic, rhythmic dialect of early Romance mixed with Euskara—voices trembling with apocalyptic terror as the turn of the millennium loomed over their fragile world.
Jose stepped from the shadows of the oaks.
"Step back," he commanded in reconstructed medieval Basque, his voice amplified by his throat-modulator, reverberating off the canyon walls like the crack of thunder.
The monks shrieked, falling prostrate into the mud. To them, Jose—with his towering frame, obsidian eyes glowing in the mist, and midnight armor that refracted the moonlight—was an archangel of wrath or a devil risen from the roots of the world.
Jose ignored their wailing and marched toward the smoking crater. But when his optical suite scanned the pit, his breath caught in his throat.
There was no meteorite.
There was only an empty fissure in the limestone, steaming from natural geothermal vents. The "celestial falling star" the monks had seen moments earlier had not been a meteor at all. It had been the blinding, superheated atmospheric rupture caused by his own temporal arrival.
A cold knot twisted in Jose’s gut. No. It had to be here.
Panic surging, he unlatched the null-cannon from his back, aiming it deep into the fissure. If there was a latent mineral deposit—some strange catalyst waiting to ignite—he would atomize the bedrock itself. He primed the core. The machine whined, a high-pitched, terrifying crescendo of antimatter capacitors charging.
"I will end this," Jose muttered, his finger tightening on the ignition stud.
He fired.
A lance of searing ultraviolet light tore into the rock. But the subterranean limestone was rich in unmapped, super-dense crystal strata. Instead of diffusing harmlessly into the earth, the null-beam reflected upward, creating a violent temporal resonance with Jose’s own chronal-displacement field.
The feedback was catastrophic.
The null-cannon violently destabilized in Jose’s hands. The energy did not dissipate; it collapsed inward, flash-melting the mountain's granite, iron veins, and the synthetic chronal alloys of his own weapon into a blinding, boiling vortex. Jose was thrown backward against the chapel wall, his suit screaming warnings of structural failure.
Before his widening eyes, the molten slag did not cool into ordinary stone. Infused with displaced 22nd-century tachyons, the matter crystallized instantly into a jagged, monumental pillar of vitreous, black, indestructible alloy that pierced the Navarre sky, humming with a low-frequency hum that vibrated the very bones in his chest.
The monks raised their tear-streaked faces from the mud. In awed terror, they watched the dark-clad visitor summon a pillar of obsidian fire from the earth. The prior, weeping, crawled toward the smoking pillar, crossing himself and chanting prayers to seal and guard this "sacred wound of God" until the end of time.
Jose lay in the mud, his hands trembling as he stared at the monolith.
The shape. The vitrified black facets. The chronal frequency buzzing in his teeth.
It was the base of the Cinder Spire.
The realization hit him like a physical blow, heavy and suffocating. The meteor had never existed. The blight had never been an accident of nature. The Spire was forged here, tonight, by the very weapon brought to destroy it.
His suit’s emergency tether began to pulse against his chest—the recall sequence was automated, unstoppable. The fabric of the year 1000 began to dissolve into streaks of blinding white.
"No!" Jose cried, clawing at the wet Pyrenean loam, trying to drag himself back toward the monument he had birthed. "No!"
The light swallowed him whole.
The return displacement spat Jose onto the cold titanium deck of the Chronos Chamber in 2150.
Technicians rushed forward, their synthetic voices a chaotic blur over the hiss of depressurizing seals. Jose shoved them aside, stumbling blindly to his feet and lunging toward the observation balcony overlooking New Pamplona.
He pressed his hands against the reinforced glass.
The sky was not clear. The air was not free.
There, dominating the horizon just as it had for centuries, stood the Cinder Spire—monolithic, black, and impenetrable. But now, as his neuro-optics zoomed in on the ancient base of the pillar, past the neon scaffolding and the Consortium’s harvest conduits, he saw what he had never understood before.
Deeply etched into the petrified alloy at the monument’s root, half-obscured by a thousand years of reverence and ruin, were the faint, fused serial numbers of an experimental 22nd-century entropic cannon—and beside them, gouged into the stone by a terrified monk's chisel in the year 1000, a crude icon of an obsidian-eyed man descending from the clouds.
Jose fell back against the glass, the mountain chill of a millennium past still clinging to the wool of his soul.
The timeline had never been broken. It had simply been waiting for him to complete it.
